-- ============================================================
-- src/test/ridgun/test operator matrix.ridgun
-- Every operator against every pair of types (FR-COMP-030): a combination
-- either gives the result written here or does not compile. None may fail,
-- or give nonsense, when the program runs.
-- Rows: kind|left type|operator|right type|ok or err|output.
--   S  `r = a op b` in `main` (Int 7 and 2, Real 7.5 and 2.0, Bool True and False, Str "ab" and "cd",
--      List [1, 2] and [3], TenU a tensor of U64 (12, 5) and (3, 2), TenR a tensor of Real (7.5, 1.5)
--      and (2.0, 0.5)); the output is `echo "{r}"`.
--   E  `a_ij op b_ij` on two tensors of that element type (U64, U8, U16, U32: (12, 5) and (3, 2));
--      the output is the two elements.
-- Each row is built and run with both backends (LLVM and the pure x86-64 encoder).
-- A new operator or type gets its rows here.
-- @tests FR-COMP-030
-- ============================================================
import str
import "io.ridgunlib"

fn :: matrix rows => Int
      matrix rows =  373

